Presidential elections were held in [[Mongolia]] on 24 May 2009. They were contested between incumbent [[Nambaryn Enkhbayar]], supported by the [[Mongolian People's Party|Mongolian People's Revolutionary Party]], and [[Tsakhiagiin Elbegdorj]], who had support of the [[Democratic Party (Mongolia)|Democratic Party]] and the opposition parties. Elbegdorj declared himself the victor before the count was official but based on his party's counts. Enkhbayar admitted a narrow defeat shortly after and said that he would respect the results, marking the second time an incumbent president had lost their bid for re-election - following [[Punsalmaagiin Ochirbat]] in the [[1997 Mongolian presidential election|1997 election]].

==Background==
There had been fears that a close result could lead to civil unrest as happened in the [[2008 Mongolian legislative election|2008 legislative election]], where allegations of vote rigging had sparked street protests and clashes with the police during which five people lost their lives and hundreds were injured.<ref name="BBC"/> Although, police and army units were on stand-by to contain any protest, the people seemed satisfied with the report of the election monitors in that there was no finding of fraudulent votes.<ref name="BBC"/><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.iar.ubc.ca/Programs/ProgramonInnerAsia/Mongolian2009Election.aspx|title=2009 Mongolian Presidential Election|date=June 1, 2009|accessdate=April 3, 2014|archive-date=July 4, 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120704180238/http://www.iar.ubc.ca/Programs/ProgramonInnerAsia/Mongolian2009Election.aspx|url-status=dead}}</ref>

==Candidates==
Presidential candidates were only allowed from parties which had representation in the [[State Great Khural]]. The eligible parties were the [[Mongolian People's Party|Mongolian People's Revolutionary Party]] (MPRP), [[Democratic Party (Mongolia)|Democratic Party]], [[Civic Will Party]] and [[Mongolian Green Party]]. The MPRP fielded the incumbent president [[Nambaryn Enkhbayar]] as its presidential candidate.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.news.mn/news/section=home/page=index#0.25167317283537183 |title=Н.Энхбаяр 100 хувийн саналаар нэр дэвшихээр боллоо |work=News.mn |date=April 3, 2009 |accessdate=April 3, 2009 |language=Mongolian |url-status=dead |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20090221123510/http://www.news.mn/news/section=home/page=index |archivedate=February 21, 2009 }}</ref> The Democratic Party chose former prime minister [[Tsakhiagiin Elbegdorj]] as their candidate.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.news.mn/news/section=home/page=index#0.4978420408013756 |title=Ц.Элбэгдорж: Манай нам ардчилсан гэдгээ харууллаа |work=News.mn |date=April 3, 2009 |accessdate=April 3, 2009 |language=Mongolian |url-status=dead |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20090221123510/http://www.news.mn/news/section=home/page=index |archivedate=February 21, 2009 }}</ref> The Civic Will and Green parties did not field a candidate and instead chose to support Tsakhiagiin Elbegdorj, the Democratic Party's candidate, thus providing the electorate with a choice between the incumbent or a candidate supported by all of the opposition parties.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.news.mn/news/section=news/page=show/content=news/id=40657|title=ИЗН Ц.Элбэгдоржийг дэмжих шийдвэрээ баталгаажууллаа|work=News.mn|date=April 3, 2009|accessdate=April 3, 2009|language=Mongolian|archive-date=April 4, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200404190819/http://www.news.mn/news/section=news/page=show/content=news/id=40657|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.news.mn/news/section=news/page=show/content=news/id=40653|title= Ногоон намын хурал дээр Ц.Элбэгдорж хөтөлбөрөө танилцуулна|work=News.mn|date= April 3, 2009|accessdate=April 3, 2009|language=Mongolian}} {{Dead link|date=October 2010|bot=H3llBot}}</ref>

Elbegdorj, who served twice as prime minister, campaigned on a theme of anti-corruption and a need for change which proved popular in urban areas.<ref name="Times">{{cite news | title = Harvard graduate Elbegdorj Tsahia wins Mongolian election|work = [[The Times]] | date = 25 May 2009 | url = http://www.timesonline.co.uk/tol/news/world/asia/article6357384.ece | archive-url = https://archive.today/20110718194627/http://www.timesonline.co.uk/tol/news/world/asia/article6357384.ece | url-status = dead | archive-date = 18 July 2011 | accessdate = 25 May 2009 | location=London | first=Jane | last=MacArtney}}</ref> Both candidates' campaigns were dominated by the issue of how Mongolia's mineral wealth should be distributed.<ref name="BBC">{{cite news | title = Mongolia opposition wins election|publisher = [[BBC]] | date = 25 May 2009 | url = https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/asia-pacific/8065608.stm | access-date = 25 May 2009}}</ref>

